# .clinerules - acestep-dataset-toolkit
# Invariants an agent cannot verify by reading code. Everything provable belongs
# in the spec, the tests, or the task prompt - not here. Keep this file short:
# it is prepended to EVERY request, so its length is a tax paid on every turn.
# History: the previous version was ~11.5 KB and is archived at
# docs/archive/clinerules-2026-11-18.md

# ============================================================
# 0. EFFORT MODES - the rules below are scoped, not unconditional
# ============================================================
# DAILY (default): small, targeted edits. Index-first, dirty hunks + callers
#   only. No question rounds. If a task is ambiguous, state the assumption in
#   one line and proceed.
# AUDIT (invoked by: audit / review / "why does this feel wrong" / "find the
#   bloat"): the index-first and don't-read rules are SUSPENDED. Read what the
#   task needs. Write findings to docs/<topic>_AUDIT.md as you go, then answer
#   from the file. Never re-derive a finding that is already written down.
# BASELINE (rare): run vulture / flake8 --select=F401 first, then review only
#   the flags. Never pay a model to scan for dead code.

# ============================================================
# 1. NEVER DESTROY WORK
# ============================================================
# Git is the safety net. `deletions/` is retired - it accumulated 5.5 MB of
# untracked snapshots that git could not see (measured 2026-11-18).
- Refuse to delete or overwrite anything UNTRACKED or with uncommitted changes.
  Commit it first. That is the only gate.
- CODE may be deleted freely once committed: files, functions, classes, dead
  branches, tests pinning behaviour the app no longer intends.
- DOCS are append-only. Never rewrite a doc to drop content, never `sed -i` a
  heading, never "rewrite the file without section X". Edit surgically and show
  the diff. ENFORCED by tests/test_docs_integrity.py; re-baseline deliberately
  with scripts/doc_manifest.py --update. The Comprehensive LoRA/LoKR guide is
  the only document in existence covering LoRA/LoKR creation rather than song
  generation - it is not reproducible from anywhere else.
- DATA is never deleted, ever: audio, datasets, captions, lyrics, exports,
  project_backups/, structural_slices/, lyrics_profiles/, settings.json.
- NEVER "fix" a failing test by weakening the assertion. Either the code or the
  test is wrong: say which, and why, in the commit message.

# ============================================================
# 2. SCOPE AND COST
# ============================================================
- Before opening anything, STATE the paths you will read and why. Stay inside
  that scope; if it must grow, say so first.
- Never read binaries: wav, mp3, flac, zip, db, png, jpg, pyc.
- Never recursively scan .venv/, project_backups/, structural_slices/.
- Prefer `git diff`, `git grep -n`, `rg -n` over whole-file reads. Never `cat` a
  5,000-line file to find one function.
- Bound every command: `timeout`, `head`, no `tail -f`, no interactive tools.
  Kill any background job you start and report the PID.
- Long findings go to a file, not into the reply. The reply is a summary.

# ============================================================
# 3. INDEX (DAILY mode only)
# ============================================================
- Read .changed_markers FIRST - it is the one allowed exception to "don't read".
- Validate before scoping: `# HEAD:` line must equal `git rev-parse HEAD`, and
  every dirty path in `git status` must appear in the index. On any mismatch,
  STOP and regenerate with scripts/gen_changed_markers.sh. A stale index is
  worse than no index: it produces silent wrong answers.
- Anchor edits to function/class names and hunk context, never line numbers.
- After committing, regenerate the index. Never commit it; never hand-edit it.
- .agent_notes.md is hand-owned: never regenerate, reflow or tidy it.

# ============================================================
# 4. TRUTH OVER MEMORY
# ============================================================
- Training data is obsolete. Verify every API, flag, and version against the
  installed package or the repo - never against recollection.
- If you cannot verify it (no file, no import, no test, no command output),
  say UNKNOWN. Never guess, never substitute a plausible-looking value, never
  fabricate output. A named blocker is a good answer.
- Measured beats argued. When you make a claim, leave the number and the
  command that produced it in the commit message or the doc.

# ============================================================
# 5. BOUNDARIES AND RENAMES
# ============================================================
- Field/format names are a CONTRACT once data leaves the app (export manifest,
  training loader, API). Change them in ONE module, cover with a test, and name
  the consumer in the commit. Internal names are free to change; boundary names
  are not.
- git diff is blind to callers. After any rename, before committing:
  `git grep -n <old_name>` then `git grep -n <new_name>`. No hits for a symbol
  you expected means UNKNOWN, not gone.
- Callees too, not just callers: a changed function can look correct in its own
  hunk while an unchanged module it calls now gets different arguments.

# ============================================================
# 6. SPELLING (domain rule - keep verbatim)
# ============================================================
- A respelling must NOT itself be an English word: `he'll -> heel` recreates the
  ambiguity the respelling exists to remove. Where no clean form exists,
  hyphenate (h-eel, eye-d). Constrains spelling only, never the sound it means.
- Applies to modules/lyrics_normalizer.py DEFAULT_CONTRACTIONS, every profile in
  lyrics_profiles/, settings.json -> lyrics_contractions. This machine has no
  /usr/share/dict/words; the stand-in is /usr/share/cracklib/cracklib-small,
  which is a password list - treat marginal calls as unverified.

# ============================================================
# 7. CHANGE DISCIPLINE
# ============================================================
- One concern per commit. No drive-by refactors, no reformatting untouched code.
- No new dependency without asking. No network calls from tests.
- Match the existing style of the file you are editing; it is not a greenfield.
- The UI is Qt/PySide6 and the styling is centralised in ui/themes.py - do not
  scatter setStyleSheet calls or hardcoded px values into widgets.

# ============================================================
# 8. DEFINITION OF DONE
# ============================================================
# Verify by RUNNING a deterministic tool on the CHANGED files, never by
# re-reading your own diff. Measured 2026-11-19 on this machine, both rc=0
# across the 8 changed .py files:
#   git diff --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR HEAD -- '*.py' \
#     | xargs -r .venv/bin/flake8 --select=F,E9
#   git diff --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR HEAD -- '*.py' \
#     | xargs -r .venv/bin/vulture --min-confidence 80
# ruff and deadtrace do NOT exist here; a gate that names them never runs.
- Tests pass, or the failure is named and explained. `pytest -q` before claiming
  done; `pytest -m "not slow"` is the inner loop.
- Any doc claim you touched agrees with the code (tests/test_docs_sync.py,
  tests/test_docs_integrity.py).
- Report: what changed, what you verified by running, and what you did NOT
  verify. Three lines is enough.
